      "headline": "The Three-Color Flavor Switch",
      "keyInsight": "We assume our feelings are deep personal secrets or complex emotional sagas. In reality, the mind stamps everything with one of three simple tags within milliseconds: pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral. It works just like a barcode scanner at a supermarket checkout. The moment sensory data arrives, the scanner beeps and tags it. If something feels good, we scramble to grab more. If it feels bad, we push it away in anger. If it feels boring, we fall asleep or daydream. Here is the funny twist: the feeling is not the real trap at all. The real trap is the immediate, automated knee-jerk story we paste on top of it. A pleasant taste is just a pleasant taste. It does not demand that you eat five more slices of cake. When you spot the raw physical sensation right as it beeps, the automatic craving breaks. You stop being a puppet dancing on three invisible strings.",
      "content": "Think of your brain as a tiny restaurant kitchen running at full speed. Before any plate leaves the counter, the head chef slaps a quick sticky note on it. That note says only one of three words: good, bad, or neither. Long before you decide whether you love your new neighbor or hate your morning commute, your system has already categorized the raw sensation. A cool glass of water on a hot day gets a green sticker. A stubbed toe on a coffee table gets a bright red sticker. The quiet humming of the refrigerator gets a dull gray sticker that you completely tune out.\n\nHere is where the hidden loop catches us off guard. We do not just register the green sticker; we instantly build an entire fortress around it. Pleasant feelings spark immediate attachment. We want to bottle the moment, freeze the clock, and replay it forever. Unpleasant feelings trigger an instant battle reflex. We grit our teeth, look around for someone to blame, and plot our escape. Neutral feelings make our attention drift into mental fog because the mind assumes nothing interesting is happening.\n\nBhikkhu Analayo points out that mindfulness steps in right at the cash register. Instead of chasing the green sticker or kicking the red one, you simply watch the scanner beep. You observe the pleasant sensation arise, peak, and fade away like ocean foam. You see unpleasant pain as simple tightness without turning it into a dramatic personal tragedy. Once you separate the raw physical feeling from your mental reaction, the chain snaps. You finally get to sit in the driver's seat of your own day.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Feelings are just quick mental traffic signals; you do not have to obey every green light or fight every red light.",
